<!-- coding-principles:start -->
## Coding principles

Examples, counter-examples, and the decisions behind these rules: `docs/coding-principles.md`.

### Rules
- No plan, spec, or task references in comments: `no-warning-comments` in `eslint.config.js`.
- New API code is plain exported functions in `src/api/` that call `request()` directly; don't add per-resource client classes like `InvoicesClient` that only forward to it. Example: `src/api/users.ts:9`.
- Name API reads `list<Resources>()` and `get<Resource>(id)`; don't use `fetch*` or `retrieve*`. Example: `src/api/users.ts:9`.
- No `*Manager` or `*Helper` type names: `@typescript-eslint/naming-convention` in `eslint.config.js`.
- Validate at the edges: HTTP responses in `request()`, environment in `src/config.ts`, where missing required config fails at startup instead of defaulting. Past that, don't null-check what the types guarantee. Example: `src/lib/http.ts:9`.
- No catch blocks that only rethrow: `no-useless-catch` in `eslint.config.js`.
- Only `src/lib/http.ts` calls fetch: `no-restricted-globals` in `eslint.config.js`.
- Read process.env only in `src/config.ts`: `no-restricted-properties` in `eslint.config.js`.
- Don't import `src/legacy/fetch-helper.ts`: `no-restricted-imports` in `eslint.config.js`.

### Canonical modules
- HTTP client: `src/lib/http.ts` (`request()`); don't add another.
- Config and environment: `src/config.ts` (`config`); don't add another.
- Errors: `src/lib/errors.ts` (`HttpError`); don't add another.
- Money: `src/lib/money.ts` (`Money`, `parseMoney()`); don't add another.

### When designing
When designing a feature or writing a spec, use the `applying-coding-principles` skill. The spec must include its section under the exact heading "Coding principles applied". When planning, copy every line of that section verbatim into the plan's Global Constraints. If a spec lacks the section, run `applying-coding-principles` before planning.

### Severity
Breaking a rule in this guide is an Important finding.
<!-- coding-principles:end -->
